% This is a placeholder for the excitation generator Simulink subsystem.
% In a real MATLAB/Simulink environment, this would be a graphical subsystem
% generating the excitation pulse train with parameterized amplitude, width, and frequency.

% The subsystem should generate:
% - Excitation pulse train based on amplitude, width, and period parameters
% - Pulses should be synchronized with sensing windows
% - Should support both fixed and adaptive excitation modes
%
% Inputs:
% - Excitation amplitude (A)
% - Excitation width (s)
% - Excitation period (s) or frequency (Hz)
% - Sync signal for sensing window alignment
% - Enable/disable control
%
% Outputs:
% - Excitation current signal
% - Excitation trigger signal (for sensing synchronization)
% - Pulse indicator (high during pulse)

% The excitation signal can be generated as:
% - Pulse width: width_samples = round(width_s * DAQ_sampling_rate)
% - Pulse amplitude: amplitude_A (scaled appropriately)
% - Pulse period: period_s = 1/frequency_Hz
% - Generate rectangular pulses at the specified frequency
%
% With adaptive control:
% - Amplitude, width, and/or period can be varied cycle-to-cycle
% - Based on uncertainty from parameter estimator
% - Within safety limits (max amplitude, max duty cycle)
%
% For sensing synchronization:
% - Trigger signal aligned with pulse start or specific sensing window
% - Accounts for propagation delays and settling times

% This file represents the intended structure. Actual implementation requires
% using MATLAB Simulink editor to create the graphical subsystem with:
% - Pulse generator blocks (rectangular pulse)
% - Gain blocks for amplitude scaling
% - Counter or timer blocks for period control
% - Logic blocks for adaptive control and safety limits
% - Output ports for excitation signal and synchronization triggers